<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pohjolanmaa (Alpo Noponen)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Finnish translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Southern Sámi version (Sami laulotasah songbook)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1911</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation, recorded by Sveriges Radio in 1938</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Verses beginning &quot;Jag älskar dig Sverige&quot; (Carl Fredrik Lundqvist)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">added third verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Verses beginning &quot;Jag älskar min hembygd&quot; (Frans Österblom)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">added verses</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Orchestral arrangement (Edvin Kallstenius)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1933</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kärestans död</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">early 1840s (as recorded by Rosa Wretman)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the melody</span></li></ul></section>
